            </section>
            <section>
               <title value="Hospital course note"/>
               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, thank you for referring your 64-year-old male patient, admitted on 30/01/2017 with an acute exacerbation of known COPD. On admission, he presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Physical examination revealed diminished breath sounds with diffuse expiratory wheezing. Arterial blood gas analysis confirmed hypoxaemia with mild hypercapnia. Chest X-ray showed pulmonary hyperinflation consistent with COPD, without evidence of pneumonia or pneumothorax. Blood tests demonstrated mild inflammatory markers. Treatment consisted of supplemental oxygen therapy, nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, and intravenous fluids. The patient responded well, with significant symptomatic improvement and stabilisation of oxygen saturation. He was discharged on 31/01/2017 with optimised inhaler therapy and a short course of oral steroids. We recommend close outpatient follow-up and pulmonary rehabilitation referral.</div>
               </text>
            </section>
